1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,480

2
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:07,600
OK, let's get started.

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:11,800
So now, let's talk a bit
about a recap of the online RL

4
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:14,040
methods that we've
talked about, so far.

5
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:18,400
So we started with talking
about policy gradients, where

6
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:20,920
our goal was to collect
data from our policy,

7
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:25,440
then increase the
likelihood on actions that

8
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:28,380
led to above average outcomes.

9
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:29,880
So encouraging the
policy to do more

10
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:31,540
of the stuff that
led to good outcomes,

11
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:33,920
less of the stuff that
led to bad outcomes.

12
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:39,780
And then we started introducing
this notion of a value function,

13
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:41,240
which can then estimate.

14
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:44,640
We can use a value function to
estimate how good an action is,

15
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:47,100
and then when we fit
a value function,

16
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:51,040
we can use that to then
encourage the policy

17
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:53,840
to do more of what you estimated
to be good and less of what you

18
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:55,760
estimated to be bad.

19
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:59,790
And we saw a couple
algorithms, like PPO and SAC,

20
00:00:59,790 --> 00:01:04,150
that use this actor
critic approach.

21
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:08,670
We also talked about a few
different ways for fitting value

22
00:01:08,670 --> 00:01:14,390
functions, and I want to quickly
go through these different ways,

23
00:01:14,390 --> 00:01:15,910
again, because
it's actually going

24
00:01:15,910 --> 00:01:19,190
to play a pretty important
role in today's lecture.

25
00:01:19,190 --> 00:01:21,470
So the first thing
we talked about

26
00:01:21,470 --> 00:01:25,950
is, if you have some
state in your data,

27
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:28,270
and you want to estimate
the value of that state,

28
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:34,270
one way to do that is to
take the sum of the rewards

29
00:01:34,270 --> 00:01:37,310
over the trajectory
in the future

30
00:01:37,310 --> 00:01:40,350
and estimate the value that way.

31
00:01:40,350 --> 00:01:47,670
So the target would be you'd be
trying to fit some V pi of S,

32
00:01:47,670 --> 00:01:52,590
and your target would be the
sum of rewards from that state

33
00:01:52,590 --> 00:01:53,210
onward.

34
00:01:53,210 --> 00:02:01,110
So you'd be trying
to contrast this

35
00:02:01,110 --> 00:02:08,710
with the sum of rewards
at future time steps--

36
00:02:08,710 --> 00:02:10,990
from future time
steps, from t to t.

37
00:02:10,990 --> 00:02:15,510
And then your objective
for your value function

38
00:02:15,510 --> 00:02:18,310
would look something like
this, where you're then

39
00:02:18,310 --> 00:02:27,190
trying to minimize this over the
states and actions in your data

40
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:27,690
set.

41
00:02:27,690 --> 00:02:30,510

42
00:02:30,510 --> 00:02:31,950
So that was the
first simplest way

43
00:02:31,950 --> 00:02:34,630
that we saw to estimate
a value function.

44
00:02:34,630 --> 00:02:37,270
And then we saw a way
to potentially get

45
00:02:37,270 --> 00:02:38,030
a better estimate.

46
00:02:38,030 --> 00:02:40,610
Because instead of just
summing all the rewards,

47
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:43,150
you could actually look at
your estimate of the value

48
00:02:43,150 --> 00:02:45,190
at an intermediate state.

49
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:50,070
So say that this is S prime
is your next state right here.

50
00:02:50,070 --> 00:02:53,170
Instead of looking at the
sum of rewards from there,

51
00:02:53,170 --> 00:02:56,140
instead you could look at what
is the reward at the next time

52
00:02:56,140 --> 00:02:57,520
step plus the value--

53
00:02:57,520 --> 00:02:59,820
my estimate for the value
at the next time step.

54
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:01,740
And use that as
your target, where

55
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:03,860
instead of being
the sum of rewards,

56
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:10,460
the target would be the reward
at the current time step

57
00:03:10,460 --> 00:03:18,490
plus the estimated value
at the next time step.

58
00:03:18,490 --> 00:03:21,100

59
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:26,940
And this is alternative target
for fitting a value function.

60
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:29,610
And so the full
objective would be--

61
00:03:29,610 --> 00:03:30,110
whoops.

62
00:03:30,110 --> 00:03:34,420

63
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:39,460
The full objective would
be something like this.

64
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:49,440
And we're trying to minimize
this over our data set.

65
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:53,940
We could say our value
function has parameters, phi.

66
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:57,380
We called this bootstrapping or
TD updates, temporal difference

67
00:03:57,380 --> 00:03:59,238
updates.

68
00:03:59,238 --> 00:04:01,780
So that was the second way that
we saw for estimating a value

69
00:04:01,780 --> 00:04:03,100
function.

70
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:05,220
And then we also
saw a third way.

71
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:11,380
So in these two
scenarios, the data set

72
00:04:11,380 --> 00:04:13,800
is sampled from your policy.

73
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:16,540
These were on policy
methods for estimating

74
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:18,860
the value of a policy.

75
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:21,860
So these were scenarios,
where your data

76
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:28,060
set was sampled from the policy
that you're trying to evaluate.

77
00:04:28,060 --> 00:04:32,260
And we then started to look
at a scenario, where what

78
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:40,380
if your data set is sampled from
some mixture of old policies?

79
00:04:40,380 --> 00:04:45,020
Could we use that data in
order to fit a value function?

80
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:50,000
And to do that, we started
looking at Q-functions.

81
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:55,610

82
00:04:55,610 --> 00:04:57,650
And with Q-functions, we
can again use something

83
00:04:57,650 --> 00:05:00,290
like bootstrapping,
where in principle, we

84
00:05:00,290 --> 00:05:06,890
could use something like Q
hat pi of phi of s t, a t,

85
00:05:06,890 --> 00:05:08,610
we're trying to estimate this.

86
00:05:08,610 --> 00:05:13,370
And then we could also
use this as our target,

87
00:05:13,370 --> 00:05:15,370
with the caveat that
this value function is

88
00:05:15,370 --> 00:05:18,410
going to be for the
policy and the data

89
00:05:18,410 --> 00:05:23,870
and not for the policy
that we're trying to learn

90
00:05:23,870 --> 00:05:26,570
and the policy that we're trying
to learn a value function for.

91
00:05:26,570 --> 00:05:31,730
And so what we did instead
is we changed our target

92
00:05:31,730 --> 00:05:35,490
to use the Q-function.

93
00:05:35,490 --> 00:05:43,030
So we had the target as
s t plus 1 a t plus 1.

94
00:05:43,030 --> 00:05:45,730

95
00:05:45,730 --> 00:05:51,030
And the key difference
here and s and a,

96
00:05:51,030 --> 00:05:52,930
and s t plus 1 are
sampled fro the data

97
00:05:52,930 --> 00:05:53,650
Set.

98
00:05:53,650 --> 00:05:58,770
But a t plus 1 is going to
be sampled from the policy

99
00:05:58,770 --> 00:06:00,830
that we're trying to learn
a value function for.

100
00:06:00,830 --> 00:06:09,450

101
00:06:09,450 --> 00:06:10,230
Cool.

102
00:06:10,230 --> 00:06:12,770
So that's a quick recap
of some of the things

103
00:06:12,770 --> 00:06:14,970
that we talked about.

104
00:06:14,970 --> 00:06:18,410
And these are the different
ways of estimating

105
00:06:18,410 --> 00:06:21,650
value functions, with the key
difference of this last one,

106
00:06:21,650 --> 00:06:27,250
being something that we can
use data from old policies for.

107
00:06:27,250 --> 00:06:32,210
Now, one really important
aspect of this last one

108
00:06:32,210 --> 00:06:35,570
is that because we're
bootstrapping and using

109
00:06:35,570 --> 00:06:38,090
our own estimate of
the value function

110
00:06:38,090 --> 00:06:41,210
at a given state
as the label, we

111
00:06:41,210 --> 00:06:48,040
need this Q-function to be
trained on actions that actually

112
00:06:48,040 --> 00:06:49,760
would resemble the
actions that we're

113
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:51,580
going to be evaluating it on.

114
00:06:51,580 --> 00:06:54,000
And so if our policy is
very different from our set

115
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:58,400
of old policies, this
label will not be accurate.

116
00:06:58,400 --> 00:07:02,280
And then we won't be able to
learn an accurate Q-function.

117
00:07:02,280 --> 00:07:05,480
So it needs good
coverage over action.

118
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:06,640
Cool.

119
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:08,880
And then those are
the different ways

120
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:10,180
of learning value functions.

121
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:13,480
The full off-policy
actor-critic method

122
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:18,820
looks something like this, where
we took data from our policy,

123
00:07:18,820 --> 00:07:21,520
stored that in the buffer
that had a number of data

124
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:23,280
from a number of past policies.

125
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:25,960
Sampled some data
from that buffer,

126
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:29,120
updated our Q-function
with these targets,

127
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:31,680
and then updated our
policy to encourage

128
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:36,560
it to increase the likelihood on
actions that had a high Q-value.

129
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:39,000
And then we can
repeat that process.

130
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,382
So for today, we're going to be
building on some of those ideas.

131
00:07:43,382 --> 00:07:44,840
And specifically,
we're going to be

132
00:07:44,840 --> 00:07:47,200
talking about offline
reinforcement learning.

133
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:50,660
And in offline
reinforcement learning,

134
00:07:50,660 --> 00:07:56,200
we're trying to learn from a
data set that was given to us,

135
00:07:56,200 --> 00:07:58,482
not collected by our own policy.

136
00:07:58,482 --> 00:08:00,440
And we'll then talk about
two different methods

137
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:02,120
or two different
classes of methods

138
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:04,520
for doing offline
reinforcement learning.

139
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:07,200
And the key things that
we'll try to see is--

140
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:09,200
I talked about is, first,
the key challenge that

141
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:10,800
arises in offline RL.

142
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:13,280
Second, two different
approaches for offline

143
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:15,000
RL and why they work.

144
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,080
And lastly, how offline
reinforcement learning

145
00:08:17,080 --> 00:08:19,480
can improve over just
imitation learning, which

146
00:08:19,480 --> 00:08:22,280
is another way to learn
from offline data sets.

147
00:08:22,280 --> 00:08:23,780
The question is,
is this Q-learning?

148
00:08:23,780 --> 00:08:26,200
This is actually
two lectures ago.

149
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:28,680
This is soft actor-critic.

150
00:08:28,680 --> 00:08:34,799
In Q-learning. , there is
one last change to this.

151
00:08:34,799 --> 00:08:39,120
So in this case, we're trying to
learn Q-function for the policy

152
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:40,640
that we're learning.

153
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:43,320
The difference that
we saw in Q-learning

154
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:45,550
was we were actually trying
to learn the Q-function

155
00:08:45,550 --> 00:08:47,230
for the optimal policy.

156
00:08:47,230 --> 00:08:58,670
And in Q-learning-- this
red marker is not great--

157
00:08:58,670 --> 00:09:00,830
our target was instead--

158
00:09:00,830 --> 00:09:02,670
instead of sampling
from the policy

159
00:09:02,670 --> 00:09:04,670
that we're trying to learn
a value function for,

160
00:09:04,670 --> 00:09:08,670
we tried to pick an action that
was the best action according

161
00:09:08,670 --> 00:09:09,830
to that Q-function.

162
00:09:09,830 --> 00:09:18,010
And so our target was
of s t, a t, plus gamma.

163
00:09:18,010 --> 00:09:23,750
And then we were taking a
max over the next action

164
00:09:23,750 --> 00:09:30,350
so that we were
actually trying to--

165
00:09:30,350 --> 00:09:35,470
and our goal here was to
try to learn the Q-function

166
00:09:35,470 --> 00:09:38,210
for the optimal policy.

167
00:09:38,210 --> 00:09:39,710
And this was also
something that can

168
00:09:39,710 --> 00:09:44,170
be done off policy, without
data from the optimal policy,

169
00:09:44,170 --> 00:09:48,270
as long as your estimates
have good coverage.

170
00:09:48,270 --> 00:09:51,430
So in offline
reinforcement learning--

171
00:09:51,430 --> 00:09:53,450
first, why offline
reinforcement learning?

172
00:09:53,450 --> 00:09:57,750
So the process for offline for
online reinforcement learning

173
00:09:57,750 --> 00:09:58,810
is, you collect data.

174
00:09:58,810 --> 00:10:00,910
Then, you update
the policy, either

175
00:10:00,910 --> 00:10:04,590
using the latest data
or the data, so far.

176
00:10:04,590 --> 00:10:06,703
And then you repeat
that process.

177
00:10:06,703 --> 00:10:08,870
With offline RL, you're
actually just given the data

178
00:10:08,870 --> 00:10:11,190
set in advance.

179
00:10:11,190 --> 00:10:13,030
And then you want
to train a policy

180
00:10:13,030 --> 00:10:15,390
on that provided data set.

181
00:10:15,390 --> 00:10:17,680
So you're not actually
iteratively collecting data.

182
00:10:17,680 --> 00:10:20,190

183
00:10:20,190 --> 00:10:21,950
And there's a number
of circumstances

184
00:10:21,950 --> 00:10:23,710
where offline reinforcement
learning might

185
00:10:23,710 --> 00:10:28,430
be more useful than online
reinforcement learning.

186
00:10:28,430 --> 00:10:31,730
The first is that when you're
trying to approach a problem,

187
00:10:31,730 --> 00:10:33,350
you might already
have a data set that

188
00:10:33,350 --> 00:10:36,950
was collected by an existing
system or by people,

189
00:10:36,950 --> 00:10:42,220
and collecting data from your
current policy might be risky

190
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:43,940
or might be unsafe.

191
00:10:43,940 --> 00:10:46,100
So, for example,
if you're trying

192
00:10:46,100 --> 00:10:49,020
to learn a policy
for controlling a car

193
00:10:49,020 --> 00:10:51,540
to drive on roads,
you might have

194
00:10:51,540 --> 00:10:53,540
a lot of data of
people driving, that

195
00:10:53,540 --> 00:10:55,580
could be very
useful for learning

196
00:10:55,580 --> 00:10:57,780
a good policy for driving.

197
00:10:57,780 --> 00:11:00,180
And it might be
unsafe to actually try

198
00:11:00,180 --> 00:11:03,260
to deploy your policy,
especially a policy that

199
00:11:03,260 --> 00:11:07,100
is not very good at driving.

200
00:11:07,100 --> 00:11:09,157
In other circumstances,
people have

201
00:11:09,157 --> 00:11:10,740
looked at using
reinforcement learning

202
00:11:10,740 --> 00:11:14,620
for figuring out good
ways to treat people

203
00:11:14,620 --> 00:11:16,220
in medical scenarios.

204
00:11:16,220 --> 00:11:18,660
So, for example, there
was a really cool project

205
00:11:18,660 --> 00:11:24,010
on a seizure
prevention use case,

206
00:11:24,010 --> 00:11:26,260
where you're trying to learn
a policy for figuring out

207
00:11:26,260 --> 00:11:27,780
how to prevent seizures.

208
00:11:27,780 --> 00:11:29,940
This is a scenario
where if you have

209
00:11:29,940 --> 00:11:33,180
an offline method of some
existing system, then

210
00:11:33,180 --> 00:11:35,980
you could use that to learn
a policy, without having

211
00:11:35,980 --> 00:11:38,700
to collect data
from a policy that

212
00:11:38,700 --> 00:11:42,660
is not good in the first place.

213
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:44,500
And then the last
point, which is actually

214
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:48,420
a little bit more nuanced,
is that if you actually

215
00:11:48,420 --> 00:11:51,380
run online or all
multiple times,

216
00:11:51,380 --> 00:11:54,760
you might actually already have
a data set from your policies.

217
00:11:54,760 --> 00:11:56,260
And it would be
really nice to reuse

218
00:11:56,260 --> 00:11:59,780
that data set rather than
trying to recollect data

219
00:11:59,780 --> 00:12:04,125
every single time you
try to run an experiment.

220
00:12:04,125 --> 00:12:06,500
If, for example, you're training
a robot to do something,

221
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:09,020
and you want it to learn
how to grasp something,

222
00:12:09,020 --> 00:12:11,775
then if you have a
pure online RL method,

223
00:12:11,775 --> 00:12:14,400
it means that every single time
you need to run your algorithm,

224
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:17,860
you need to recollect
data from your policy.

225
00:12:17,860 --> 00:12:20,900
And that can be pretty
inefficient, because probably,

226
00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:23,540
the first time you run
the algorithm isn't--

227
00:12:23,540 --> 00:12:27,540
you still room for iteration
on the algorithm itself.

228
00:12:27,540 --> 00:12:31,740
And generally, online data is
more expensive than offline data

229
00:12:31,740 --> 00:12:36,570
for that reason, because offline
data, you can reuse many times.

230
00:12:36,570 --> 00:12:38,970
Also worth noting that
you can blend offline

231
00:12:38,970 --> 00:12:41,430
RL and online RL, where you
have some existing data set.

232
00:12:41,430 --> 00:12:43,490
Like in autonomous driving,
you have a data set

233
00:12:43,490 --> 00:12:47,150
of people driving, and then you
want to train a policy on that.

234
00:12:47,150 --> 00:12:49,610
And then you try to improve
it with some online data

235
00:12:49,610 --> 00:12:52,330
collection, perhaps
with a safety driver

236
00:12:52,330 --> 00:12:54,890
after you have a policy
that's pretty good.

237
00:12:54,890 --> 00:12:58,690
And there's a number of
real-world use cases, where

238
00:12:58,690 --> 00:13:00,190
you will want to blend it.

239
00:13:00,190 --> 00:13:02,310
Because if you actually
are deploying your policy,

240
00:13:02,310 --> 00:13:04,210
then it's great to
be able to improve it

241
00:13:04,210 --> 00:13:06,310
after you deploy your policy.

242
00:13:06,310 --> 00:13:06,810
Yeah.

243
00:13:06,810 --> 00:13:09,610
So the question is, can you view
offline RL as a special case

244
00:13:09,610 --> 00:13:12,930
of online, but
off-policy RL, but where

245
00:13:12,930 --> 00:13:14,170
you don't know the data.

246
00:13:14,170 --> 00:13:18,922
And that's definitely
one that's very--

247
00:13:18,922 --> 00:13:21,970
that's a correct way to view it.

248
00:13:21,970 --> 00:13:24,650
One thing that is a very strict
requirement in offline RL,

249
00:13:24,650 --> 00:13:27,210
in pure offline RL, is
that you have no ability

250
00:13:27,210 --> 00:13:28,890
to collect more data.

251
00:13:28,890 --> 00:13:30,483
And in that sense,
it's not really

252
00:13:30,483 --> 00:13:31,650
a special case of online RL.

253
00:13:31,650 --> 00:13:35,850
Because in online off-policy
RL, you can collect more data.

254
00:13:35,850 --> 00:13:38,690
That said, we'll see that
some of the techniques

255
00:13:38,690 --> 00:13:42,130
will draw upon some of
these off policy techniques

256
00:13:42,130 --> 00:13:43,850
from an algorithm standpoint.

257
00:13:43,850 --> 00:13:47,090
Because in these
scenarios, you're

258
00:13:47,090 --> 00:13:49,530
able to use data
from old policies,

259
00:13:49,530 --> 00:13:51,570
and that's very
useful when you have

260
00:13:51,570 --> 00:13:54,610
an offline data set from some
policy that you don't know.

261
00:13:54,610 --> 00:13:58,590
So now let's talk about this
a little bit more formally.

262
00:13:58,590 --> 00:14:04,270
So say that you're
given some data set,

263
00:14:04,270 --> 00:14:12,990
and the data set, maybe,
has some trajectories in it.

264
00:14:12,990 --> 00:14:14,970
Maybe if it's a
driving scenario,

265
00:14:14,970 --> 00:14:22,365
then you have some car, and you
have data sets of people driving

266
00:14:22,365 --> 00:14:24,990
in a number of different ways,
in a number of different places,

267
00:14:24,990 --> 00:14:27,010
with a number of different cars.

268
00:14:27,010 --> 00:14:34,640
And we're going to say
that this constitutes

269
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:37,280
some data set that we're given.

270
00:14:37,280 --> 00:14:43,320
And it's helpful to formally
think about the policy that

271
00:14:43,320 --> 00:14:44,700
collected this data.

272
00:14:44,700 --> 00:14:46,780
Now, we don't know
the underlying policy.

273
00:14:46,780 --> 00:14:49,480
So for example, if this
was collected by people,

274
00:14:49,480 --> 00:14:51,378
we don't know what--

275
00:14:51,378 --> 00:14:53,420
well, first, it was
collected by multiple people,

276
00:14:53,420 --> 00:14:55,120
then it's multiple policies.

277
00:14:55,120 --> 00:14:57,920
And we also just don't
directly have access to that.

278
00:14:57,920 --> 00:15:00,200
But it's useful to
think about that policy,

279
00:15:00,200 --> 00:15:02,460
just from a
mathematical standpoint.

280
00:15:02,460 --> 00:15:06,400
And so we'll say that this
data set was collected

281
00:15:06,400 --> 00:15:13,240
by running some policy that will
refer to as the behavior policy

282
00:15:13,240 --> 00:15:14,050
or pi beta.

283
00:15:14,050 --> 00:15:16,800

284
00:15:16,800 --> 00:15:20,660
And if it's collected
by multiple policies,

285
00:15:20,660 --> 00:15:24,760
then you can think of pi beta
as a mixture of policies.

286
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:32,720
And you can define that mixture
as some average or weighted sum

287
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:36,040
over some constituent policies.

288
00:15:36,040 --> 00:15:39,000
So we don't lose any
generality by saying that it's

289
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:40,210
collected by a single policy.

290
00:15:40,210 --> 00:15:43,080

291
00:15:43,080 --> 00:15:43,620
Cool.

292
00:15:43,620 --> 00:15:48,960
So that's the data set sample
from some unknown policy.

293
00:15:48,960 --> 00:15:51,920
Maybe a mixture of policies.

294
00:15:51,920 --> 00:15:57,020
If we were to think about the
distribution over this data,

295
00:15:57,020 --> 00:15:59,160
we could think of it,
having some underlying state

296
00:15:59,160 --> 00:16:03,560
distribution, corresponding
to this policy, an action

297
00:16:03,560 --> 00:16:08,280
distribution dynamics,
a reward function.

298
00:16:08,280 --> 00:16:15,280
And then our goal is to
learn a good policy that

299
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:17,880
maximizes reward.

300
00:16:17,880 --> 00:16:21,280
And so ideally, we would be
able to take this data set

301
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:27,780
and be able to learn some
policy that can control the car.

302
00:16:27,780 --> 00:16:30,930
And ideally, it's maybe
a pretty good policy.

303
00:16:30,930 --> 00:16:34,110
Maybe some of the times, it
does merge into another lane.

304
00:16:34,110 --> 00:16:39,350
But generally, we want a policy
that will refer to as pi theta.

305
00:16:39,350 --> 00:16:45,070
And we want to be able
to ultimately maximize

306
00:16:45,070 --> 00:16:50,110
an objective, corresponding to
the sum of rewards for how good

307
00:16:50,110 --> 00:16:51,710
this policy is.

308
00:16:51,710 --> 00:16:54,310
And so you can think
about that as maximizing

309
00:16:54,310 --> 00:16:56,990
the parameters of
this policy and trying

310
00:16:56,990 --> 00:17:04,650
to maximize for trajectories
sampled from this policy,

311
00:17:04,650 --> 00:17:07,960
maximizing the sum of rewards.

312
00:17:07,960 --> 00:17:11,230

313
00:17:11,230 --> 00:17:14,430
Or, well, either--

314
00:17:14,430 --> 00:17:16,369
I guess I'll write it
as r of r of trajectory,

315
00:17:16,369 --> 00:17:21,150
where this is the sum of
rewards for individual states

316
00:17:21,150 --> 00:17:22,290
in that trajectory.

317
00:17:22,290 --> 00:17:25,589

318
00:17:25,589 --> 00:17:30,790
Now, one thing to note
here is that this objective

319
00:17:30,790 --> 00:17:34,590
is an expectation
over this policy.

320
00:17:34,590 --> 00:17:39,190
Whereas, our data was collected
by a different policy.

321
00:17:39,190 --> 00:17:42,070
And so we can think about this
as there being some distribution

322
00:17:42,070 --> 00:17:44,190
shift between the
policy that collected

323
00:17:44,190 --> 00:17:46,870
the data and the policy
that we're deploying.

324
00:17:46,870 --> 00:17:49,990
And we don't actually get
to sample from that policy.

325
00:17:49,990 --> 00:17:52,310
And so that's one of
the big challenges

326
00:17:52,310 --> 00:17:56,230
in offline
reinforcement learning.

327
00:17:56,230 --> 00:17:57,830
So there's this
distribution shift

328
00:17:57,830 --> 00:18:00,750
because this expectation
is under the learned policy

329
00:18:00,750 --> 00:18:04,150
rather than under the policy
that collected the data.

330
00:18:04,150 --> 00:18:06,590
And then it's worth
mentioning that the dynamics

331
00:18:06,590 --> 00:18:08,230
of the environment
are going to be

332
00:18:08,230 --> 00:18:12,850
constant between the data
and the learned policy.

333
00:18:12,850 --> 00:18:16,090
So the distribution shift is
only for these trajectories.

334
00:18:16,090 --> 00:18:20,150
It's only caused by the
shift in the policy.

335
00:18:20,150 --> 00:18:23,237
The question is, if the data
set is comprehensive enough,

336
00:18:23,237 --> 00:18:24,820
can it turn out that
the learned learn

337
00:18:24,820 --> 00:18:28,400
policy and the behavior policy
distribution are identical?

338
00:18:28,400 --> 00:18:31,860

339
00:18:31,860 --> 00:18:35,308
Generally, that's
extremely rare.

340
00:18:35,308 --> 00:18:36,600
Generally, that doesn't happen.

341
00:18:36,600 --> 00:18:43,940
And the reason is that,
usually-- well, first, you

342
00:18:43,940 --> 00:18:46,380
often want to improve
over your data set

343
00:18:46,380 --> 00:18:50,140
and learn something that's
better than the average driver.

344
00:18:50,140 --> 00:18:52,242
And so in that case,
you might actually

345
00:18:52,242 --> 00:18:53,700
end up with a
narrower distribution

346
00:18:53,700 --> 00:18:56,740
here than your original policy.

347
00:18:56,740 --> 00:18:59,220
In the case where your
original policy is very good,

348
00:18:59,220 --> 00:19:02,680
then you're somewhat more in an
imitation learning like setting,

349
00:19:02,680 --> 00:19:05,580
and you might still
diverge and drift away

350
00:19:05,580 --> 00:19:12,350
from the distribution
in the data set.

351
00:19:12,350 --> 00:19:13,600
Where does the data come from?

352
00:19:13,600 --> 00:19:16,260
It can be human collected
from some hand-designed system

353
00:19:16,260 --> 00:19:18,180
or controller.

354
00:19:18,180 --> 00:19:21,260
It could also be from previous
reinforcement learning runs,

355
00:19:21,260 --> 00:19:22,240
that you want to reuse.

356
00:19:22,240 --> 00:19:24,220
It could be a mixture
of these sources.

357
00:19:24,220 --> 00:19:27,040
Lots of different places
the data could come from.

358
00:19:27,040 --> 00:19:29,820

359
00:19:29,820 --> 00:19:32,460
Now, there were some
comments about how

360
00:19:32,460 --> 00:19:35,300
this offline reinforcement
learning relates to off policy

361
00:19:35,300 --> 00:19:36,620
reinforcement learning.

362
00:19:36,620 --> 00:19:38,740
And one natural
question is maybe

363
00:19:38,740 --> 00:19:42,180
we could just reuse an
off-policy algorithm.

364
00:19:42,180 --> 00:19:46,920
And just run that
off-policy algorithm,

365
00:19:46,920 --> 00:19:55,740
but using the offline data set
rather than recollecting data

366
00:19:55,740 --> 00:19:58,405
with our new policy.

367
00:19:58,405 --> 00:19:59,780
Does anyone have
thoughts on what

368
00:19:59,780 --> 00:20:03,580
will happen if you use
an off policy algorithm,

369
00:20:03,580 --> 00:20:09,580
like soft actor critic
on an offline data set?

370
00:20:09,580 --> 00:20:15,300
Yeah, so the thought there
was that we have this--

371
00:20:15,300 --> 00:20:19,460
we're evaluating our
Q-estimate on actions sampled

372
00:20:19,460 --> 00:20:21,490
from our policy, and
our policy is also

373
00:20:21,490 --> 00:20:26,530
trying to increase likelihood on
Q-values on states that have--

374
00:20:26,530 --> 00:20:28,530
or on actions that
have high Q-values.

375
00:20:28,530 --> 00:20:30,433
And if we have an
offline data set,

376
00:20:30,433 --> 00:20:31,850
and we're learning
a policy that's

377
00:20:31,850 --> 00:20:36,250
different from the policy
that collected the data, then

378
00:20:36,250 --> 00:20:38,990
this Q-estimate that
we're supervising,

379
00:20:38,990 --> 00:20:42,490
we're using to supervise our
Q-function may not be accurate.

380
00:20:42,490 --> 00:20:46,770
Because it didn't
actually necessarily see

381
00:20:46,770 --> 00:20:52,450
actions in the data that come
from the policy we're learning.

382
00:20:52,450 --> 00:20:55,215
And so we might actually end up
learning an accurate Q-function.

383
00:20:55,215 --> 00:20:57,090
And if we don't learn
an accurate Q-function,

384
00:20:57,090 --> 00:21:01,410
then we might get a
bad policy as a result.

385
00:21:01,410 --> 00:21:03,930
And so to walk through
that a little bit more,

386
00:21:03,930 --> 00:21:09,363
let's say that
we, maybe, again--

387
00:21:09,363 --> 00:21:11,030
you can have a little
bit of space here.

388
00:21:11,030 --> 00:21:19,440
So let's say that we have our
data set and some of the data.

389
00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:21,190
Here are a few of the
actions in the data.

390
00:21:21,190 --> 00:21:22,570
Maybe this is a1.

391
00:21:22,570 --> 00:21:23,810
This is a2.

392
00:21:23,810 --> 00:21:25,650
This is a3.

393
00:21:25,650 --> 00:21:28,770
And there's other actions
that the car could take

394
00:21:28,770 --> 00:21:30,190
that's not in the data set.

395
00:21:30,190 --> 00:21:32,810
So for example,
maybe the car could

396
00:21:32,810 --> 00:21:34,930
go on an adventure over here.

397
00:21:34,930 --> 00:21:37,250
Maybe that's a4.

398
00:21:37,250 --> 00:21:40,130
The car could also
make a U-turn.

399
00:21:40,130 --> 00:21:42,010
That's a5.

400
00:21:42,010 --> 00:21:43,970
The car could also go backwards.

401
00:21:43,970 --> 00:21:45,490
Maybe that's a6.

402
00:21:45,490 --> 00:21:48,810
So there's all actions
that the car could do.

403
00:21:48,810 --> 00:21:51,570
And at the beginning
of training,

404
00:21:51,570 --> 00:21:56,770
we're going to be randomly
initializing our Q-function.

405
00:21:56,770 --> 00:22:02,490
And so if you think about some
randomly initialized Q-function

406
00:22:02,490 --> 00:22:08,480
for a given state, maybe
it looks somewhat random.

407
00:22:08,480 --> 00:22:13,170

408
00:22:13,170 --> 00:22:14,600
And this is for
different actions.

409
00:22:14,600 --> 00:22:18,920

410
00:22:18,920 --> 00:22:23,320
Then only some of these actions
are present in the data.

411
00:22:23,320 --> 00:22:28,000
And so maybe a1, a2,
and a3, maybe they're

412
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:31,110
all within this range.

413
00:22:31,110 --> 00:22:33,760

414
00:22:33,760 --> 00:22:37,960
So this is what's like in
distribution in the data.

415
00:22:37,960 --> 00:22:40,760
And then outside of
that, your Q-function

416
00:22:40,760 --> 00:22:43,120
is somewhat arbitrary.

417
00:22:43,120 --> 00:22:46,880
And you don't actually see
any data for outside that.

418
00:22:46,880 --> 00:22:48,937
And then when you
do a policy update,

419
00:22:48,937 --> 00:22:50,520
your policy is going
to say, oh, maybe

420
00:22:50,520 --> 00:22:55,640
let's try to increase
the likelihood on actions

421
00:22:55,640 --> 00:22:57,480
that have a high Q-value.

422
00:22:57,480 --> 00:23:04,360
This is actions a, Q of s, a.

423
00:23:04,360 --> 00:23:06,020
Then what it will do
is, it'll say, OK,

424
00:23:06,020 --> 00:23:08,800
for this randomly initialized
Q value, maybe something

425
00:23:08,800 --> 00:23:11,160
like this looks good.

426
00:23:11,160 --> 00:23:13,620
And then if that's
outside your data,

427
00:23:13,620 --> 00:23:16,845
then it will still
continue to think

428
00:23:16,845 --> 00:23:18,720
that that's a good action
because it'll never

429
00:23:18,720 --> 00:23:22,500
actually know what the proper
Q-value for that action is.

430
00:23:22,500 --> 00:23:25,400

431
00:23:25,400 --> 00:23:29,760
And so walking through that,
basically, everything--

432
00:23:29,760 --> 00:23:32,940
Maybe another way to think about
this is that here's your data.

433
00:23:32,940 --> 00:23:38,480
Here are some of the actions
that are out of distribution,

434
00:23:38,480 --> 00:23:40,800
including a4, a5.

435
00:23:40,800 --> 00:23:45,220
It never saw a car
that, for example,

436
00:23:45,220 --> 00:23:47,440
makes a U-turn on the highway.

437
00:23:47,440 --> 00:23:50,957
And so the Q-value is
randomly initialized for that.

438
00:23:50,957 --> 00:23:53,040
Some of those actions that
are out of distribution

439
00:23:53,040 --> 00:23:55,820
will arbitrarily have
higher key values.

440
00:23:55,820 --> 00:23:58,320
Some will have lower because
it's randomly initialized,

441
00:23:58,320 --> 00:24:01,600
and then your policy will
exploit those inaccuracies

442
00:24:01,600 --> 00:24:03,150
on the out of
distribution actions.

443
00:24:03,150 --> 00:24:06,120

444
00:24:06,120 --> 00:24:12,200
So on the slides, we have
this term right here.

445
00:24:12,200 --> 00:24:15,390
When you evaluate this
Q-function on a prime

446
00:24:15,390 --> 00:24:17,390
that's not in the
data set, if you

447
00:24:17,390 --> 00:24:21,710
have some randomly initialized
Q-function for that state,

448
00:24:21,710 --> 00:24:24,550
and your data support
only covers part of that,

449
00:24:24,550 --> 00:24:27,090
then on the
out-of-distribution actions,

450
00:24:27,090 --> 00:24:29,230
your Q-function
will be unreliable.

451
00:24:29,230 --> 00:24:30,970
And then when you do
your policy update,

452
00:24:30,970 --> 00:24:33,630
that's going to seek out
actions, where the Q-function is

453
00:24:33,630 --> 00:24:36,230
overoptimistic.

454
00:24:36,230 --> 00:24:37,850
And then after
the policy update,

455
00:24:37,850 --> 00:24:41,130
these key values will
be overestimated,

456
00:24:41,130 --> 00:24:43,590
because now you're going
to keep on reinforcing

457
00:24:43,590 --> 00:24:48,523
that was good behavior, even
when it's not in your data.

458
00:24:48,523 --> 00:24:50,190
Another perspective
on this is that this

459
00:24:50,190 --> 00:24:53,350
will happen in scenarios where
your learn policy is deviating

460
00:24:53,350 --> 00:24:55,500
too much from your
behavior policy.

461
00:24:55,500 --> 00:24:57,250
Because these
out-of-distribution actions.

462
00:24:57,250 --> 00:24:59,470
These are things that are very
different from what the behavior

463
00:24:59,470 --> 00:25:00,370
policy did.

464
00:25:00,370 --> 00:25:02,750
And if we stayed closer
to the behavior policy,

465
00:25:02,750 --> 00:25:06,292
then we wouldn't
have this issue.

466
00:25:06,292 --> 00:25:08,750
The question is, what happens
if you have a discrete action

467
00:25:08,750 --> 00:25:09,510
space?

468
00:25:09,510 --> 00:25:12,940
So basically, everything
will be the same.

469
00:25:12,940 --> 00:25:15,950

470
00:25:15,950 --> 00:25:17,950
It's possible that
your data set,

471
00:25:17,950 --> 00:25:20,410
if in all the states
in your data set,

472
00:25:20,410 --> 00:25:22,730
it has full coverage
of the actions,

473
00:25:22,730 --> 00:25:25,870
then you won't have this
problem because everything

474
00:25:25,870 --> 00:25:28,270
will be in distribution.

475
00:25:28,270 --> 00:25:30,510
But in practice,
usually, there will

476
00:25:30,510 --> 00:25:33,390
be states, where you don't have
full coverage of all actions.

477
00:25:33,390 --> 00:25:36,670
And you can still see
overestimation in those states.

478
00:25:36,670 --> 00:25:38,643
Yeah, so the question
is, can you just exclude

479
00:25:38,643 --> 00:25:40,310
out-of-distribution
actions, when you're

480
00:25:40,310 --> 00:25:42,630
updating your Q-function?

481
00:25:42,630 --> 00:25:45,350
Yeah, and there are
approaches for doing that.

482
00:25:45,350 --> 00:25:47,990
Now, the unfortunate
thing is we don't actually

483
00:25:47,990 --> 00:25:50,650
know what's in distribution
and out of distribution.

484
00:25:50,650 --> 00:25:54,650
So we have just samples
of states and actions.

485
00:25:54,650 --> 00:25:57,390
And for a given
state, you might have

486
00:25:57,390 --> 00:25:59,895
one state that then chooses a1.

487
00:25:59,895 --> 00:26:01,270
Then you might
have another state

488
00:26:01,270 --> 00:26:03,610
that's not exactly the
same, but very similar.

489
00:26:03,610 --> 00:26:04,770
And that takes a2.

490
00:26:04,770 --> 00:26:10,460
And so in that case, a2
might technically not

491
00:26:10,460 --> 00:26:12,040
be seen for the first state.

492
00:26:12,040 --> 00:26:13,540
But you don't
necessarily know-- you

493
00:26:13,540 --> 00:26:15,707
don't know for a given
state, what's in distribution

494
00:26:15,707 --> 00:26:18,420
and what's out of distribution.

495
00:26:18,420 --> 00:26:21,460
And yeah, we'll basically see--

496
00:26:21,460 --> 00:26:23,500
basically, a key idea
behind offline RL methods

497
00:26:23,500 --> 00:26:25,400
is trying to avoid--

498
00:26:25,400 --> 00:26:27,020
well, first trying
to think about how

499
00:26:27,020 --> 00:26:29,700
do we deviate not too much
from the behavior policy.

500
00:26:29,700 --> 00:26:34,380
And second, can we avoid
out-of-distribution actions?

501
00:26:34,380 --> 00:26:39,220
OK, so let's talk about
mitigating overestimation.

502
00:26:39,220 --> 00:26:44,180
This is really the core goal
behind offline RL methods.

503
00:26:44,180 --> 00:26:52,540
So the one approach to
offline RL is basically

504
00:26:52,540 --> 00:26:53,880
to do imitation learning.

505
00:26:53,880 --> 00:26:56,700
And the reason why
imitation learning works

506
00:26:56,700 --> 00:26:59,380
is that you're only
supervising with actions

507
00:26:59,380 --> 00:27:00,860
that are seen in the data.

508
00:27:00,860 --> 00:27:04,060
And so any approach that
uses imitation learning

509
00:27:04,060 --> 00:27:07,780
is going to be avoiding
out-of-distribution actions,

510
00:27:07,780 --> 00:27:10,060
somewhat implicitly
because you're only

511
00:27:10,060 --> 00:27:14,260
going to be supervising with
actions that are in the data.

512
00:27:14,260 --> 00:27:16,700
Now, we don't necessarily
want to use vanilla imitation

513
00:27:16,700 --> 00:27:20,363
learning because the offline
data may not be optimal.

514
00:27:20,363 --> 00:27:22,280
There might be human
drivers in your data set,

515
00:27:22,280 --> 00:27:24,660
for example, that
don't drive in the way

516
00:27:24,660 --> 00:27:28,980
that you want your
autonomous vehicle to drive.

517
00:27:28,980 --> 00:27:32,900
And offline RL methods
should provide a way

518
00:27:32,900 --> 00:27:36,180
to be better than the
policy that underlies

519
00:27:36,180 --> 00:27:39,860
the data by leveraging
reward information,

520
00:27:39,860 --> 00:27:41,820
by leveraging the
fact that maybe you

521
00:27:41,820 --> 00:27:44,260
can go through your data
set and say that this

522
00:27:44,260 --> 00:27:47,100
was a behavior that was good.

523
00:27:47,100 --> 00:27:52,158
And maybe this was a behavior
that was not as good.

524
00:27:52,158 --> 00:27:53,700
And that reward
information can allow

525
00:27:53,700 --> 00:27:57,150
you to get a better policy.

526
00:27:57,150 --> 00:27:58,900
And then, yeah, vanilla
imitation learning

527
00:27:58,900 --> 00:28:03,605
can't outperform the expert or
what's provided in the data.

528
00:28:03,605 --> 00:28:04,980
The other thing
that I'll mention

529
00:28:04,980 --> 00:28:10,090
is that I'm not sure if
there's an analogous situation.

530
00:28:10,090 --> 00:28:12,570
Maybe there is actually probably
some analogous situation

531
00:28:12,570 --> 00:28:15,410
in driving, but a good
offline RL method can also

532
00:28:15,410 --> 00:28:17,770
stitch together good behaviors.

533
00:28:17,770 --> 00:28:19,420
So if you have--

534
00:28:19,420 --> 00:28:22,730

535
00:28:22,730 --> 00:28:23,650
where to put this?

536
00:28:23,650 --> 00:28:29,370
Say you have-- maybe
let's put it over here.

537
00:28:29,370 --> 00:28:32,250
So say you have some trajectory
in your offline data set

538
00:28:32,250 --> 00:28:34,130
that looks something like this.

539
00:28:34,130 --> 00:28:39,450
And another trajectory
of your data set

540
00:28:39,450 --> 00:28:43,050
maybe looks something
like this, and say

541
00:28:43,050 --> 00:28:45,850
that this one got a
good reward at the end,

542
00:28:45,850 --> 00:28:49,610
and this one got a
negative reward at the end.

543
00:28:49,610 --> 00:28:52,010
Then a good offline RL
method should realize

544
00:28:52,010 --> 00:28:55,910
that this is a good behavior.

545
00:28:55,910 --> 00:28:58,490
And also, the first
part of this trajectory,

546
00:28:58,490 --> 00:29:03,370
if you continue along the
trajectory at the end also

547
00:29:03,370 --> 00:29:05,910
can be a good behavior
and a good offer.

548
00:29:05,910 --> 00:29:10,750
RL method should realize that
when you're in these states,

549
00:29:10,750 --> 00:29:15,250
you should essentially be
able to stitch together

550
00:29:15,250 --> 00:29:16,730
the first part of
this trajectory

551
00:29:16,730 --> 00:29:19,730
and the second part of
the green trajectory.

552
00:29:19,730 --> 00:29:22,170
And if you use vanilla
imitation learning,

553
00:29:22,170 --> 00:29:23,990
you won't get that behavior.

554
00:29:23,990 --> 00:29:26,550
Instead, what it will
do is, in this state,

555
00:29:26,550 --> 00:29:33,370
it will take the average between
these two action distributions.

556
00:29:33,370 --> 00:29:36,210
So what this could look like
is, if you have a couple

557
00:29:36,210 --> 00:29:40,562
trajectories like this,
then we know that S1 to S3

558
00:29:40,562 --> 00:29:41,270
is good behavior.

559
00:29:41,270 --> 00:29:44,330
We know S7 to S9
is good behavior.

560
00:29:44,330 --> 00:29:47,170
And so offline reinforcement
learning methods, in principle,

561
00:29:47,170 --> 00:29:52,970
can actually learn how to get a
policy that goes from S1 to S9,

562
00:29:52,970 --> 00:29:55,290
whereas, imitation
learning methods won't

563
00:29:55,290 --> 00:29:57,770
be able to do that.

564
00:29:57,770 --> 00:30:04,680
So before we get into methods
that can do that stitching,

565
00:30:04,680 --> 00:30:09,760
let's first talk about a
very simple baseline method.

566
00:30:09,760 --> 00:30:16,960
So one idea is if you
have a offline data set,

567
00:30:16,960 --> 00:30:19,880
you could also just
take the trajectories

568
00:30:19,880 --> 00:30:22,200
that have high rewards
and only imitate

569
00:30:22,200 --> 00:30:26,760
the stuff that is good and don't
imitate the stuff that's bad.

570
00:30:26,760 --> 00:30:29,720
And that seems like
a reasonable method.

571
00:30:29,720 --> 00:30:33,720
You have some offline
data set that contains

572
00:30:33,720 --> 00:30:36,560
a variety of trajectories.

573
00:30:36,560 --> 00:30:39,160
Then, what you could
do is, you could

574
00:30:39,160 --> 00:30:43,520
define a new data set that
has only trajectories that

575
00:30:43,520 --> 00:30:46,000
have a high reward.

576
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:48,840
And so what this new data
set would look like is,

577
00:30:48,840 --> 00:30:51,000
it would be all of
the trajectories

578
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:54,960
in your original data
set, except conditioned

579
00:30:54,960 --> 00:30:59,160
on the reward of that
trajectory, being greater

580
00:30:59,160 --> 00:31:01,580
than some threshold.

581
00:31:01,580 --> 00:31:05,720
And that threshold could be
some percentile of the rewards

582
00:31:05,720 --> 00:31:08,880
in your data set, like the
70th percentile, for example,

583
00:31:08,880 --> 00:31:12,440
only taking the top
30% of trajectories.

584
00:31:12,440 --> 00:31:15,400
And then from there,
you could learn

585
00:31:15,400 --> 00:31:21,680
a policy that maximizes the
likelihood of the actions

586
00:31:21,680 --> 00:31:24,160
in this modified data set.

587
00:31:24,160 --> 00:31:28,200
So that would look like
taking the states and actions

588
00:31:28,200 --> 00:31:35,040
in this modified data set and
maximizing the log probability

589
00:31:35,040 --> 00:31:37,540
of the actions given the state.

590
00:31:37,540 --> 00:31:42,280
So this is the typical
imitation learning objective.

591
00:31:42,280 --> 00:31:43,680
Cool.

592
00:31:43,680 --> 00:31:47,760
So this is, perhaps, our
first offline RL method,

593
00:31:47,760 --> 00:31:50,560
I guess, very, very basic.

594
00:31:50,560 --> 00:31:53,040
Filter the data set to do
the top k percent of data,

595
00:31:53,040 --> 00:31:55,520
and then imitate the
filtered data set.

596
00:31:55,520 --> 00:31:56,920
This is a very
primitive approach

597
00:31:56,920 --> 00:31:58,910
for using reward information.

598
00:31:58,910 --> 00:32:01,270
And it's worth
noting that it's not

599
00:32:01,270 --> 00:32:02,890
able to do this
stitching behavior.

600
00:32:02,890 --> 00:32:05,690
Because what it will do is,
if this is a bad trajectory,

601
00:32:05,690 --> 00:32:08,110
it might just filter out
that trajectory entirely,

602
00:32:08,110 --> 00:32:10,670
and it won't actually
learn how to take

603
00:32:10,670 --> 00:32:12,630
this good behavior and
this good behavior,

604
00:32:12,630 --> 00:32:17,190
and figure out how to
get from here to here.

605
00:32:17,190 --> 00:32:18,970
That said, it's a good baseline.

606
00:32:18,970 --> 00:32:21,930
So if you're going to be trying
out any offline RL method,

607
00:32:21,930 --> 00:32:25,990
it's good to try this first
and see how well it does,

608
00:32:25,990 --> 00:32:27,850
and use that as a benchmark.

609
00:32:27,850 --> 00:32:29,910
And make sure that--
or ideally, try

610
00:32:29,910 --> 00:32:32,470
to make sure that your offline
RL method is doing better

611
00:32:32,470 --> 00:32:34,390
than this.

612
00:32:34,390 --> 00:32:37,670
OK, now, one of the
reasons why I introduced

613
00:32:37,670 --> 00:32:41,292
this is that, arguably, some
of the best offline RL methods

614
00:32:41,292 --> 00:32:42,750
are actually doing
something that's

615
00:32:42,750 --> 00:32:45,270
not all that different
from this, in that they do

616
00:32:45,270 --> 00:32:48,870
have imitation learning
component to them.

617
00:32:48,870 --> 00:32:54,830
And the idea here is that
instead of just taking

618
00:32:54,830 --> 00:32:59,070
the good trajectories, we
could imitate the data,

619
00:32:59,070 --> 00:33:01,070
but weight our
imitation learning

620
00:33:01,070 --> 00:33:09,630
objective based on how good the
action was in that transition.

621
00:33:09,630 --> 00:33:13,990
Now, if you remember what
we talked about with regard

622
00:33:13,990 --> 00:33:16,250
to value functions and
advantage functions,

623
00:33:16,250 --> 00:33:19,710
where the advantage function is
how much better is a t, compared

624
00:33:19,710 --> 00:33:24,550
to what your policy does,
this advantage function

625
00:33:24,550 --> 00:33:29,430
suggests that if we weighted
our imitation learning objective

626
00:33:29,430 --> 00:33:31,348
by the advantage
function, then we

627
00:33:31,348 --> 00:33:33,390
could get some measure of
how good that action is

628
00:33:33,390 --> 00:33:38,230
and get a policy that is
able to learn effectively.

629
00:33:38,230 --> 00:33:44,170
And this will form the objective
of our first offline RL method.

630
00:33:44,170 --> 00:33:46,790
And in particular,
what we'll do is

631
00:33:46,790 --> 00:33:54,430
we will maximize an
objective for a policy that

632
00:33:54,430 --> 00:33:58,580
will be taking the
standard imitation learning

633
00:33:58,580 --> 00:34:07,072
objective, but just weighting it
by the exponentiated advantages.

634
00:34:07,072 --> 00:34:10,460

635
00:34:10,460 --> 00:34:15,300
And this will be a sum over
states and actions in our data

636
00:34:15,300 --> 00:34:17,900
set.

637
00:34:17,900 --> 00:34:21,440
In terms of the choice
to exponentiate them,

638
00:34:21,440 --> 00:34:26,699
there's actually a way to derive
this particular objective.

639
00:34:26,699 --> 00:34:28,460
And I'll mention
that on the slides.

640
00:34:28,460 --> 00:34:30,000
We won't go through
the derivation.

641
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:32,020
But if you're interested,
we can see if we

642
00:34:32,020 --> 00:34:34,900
can post it after lecture.

643
00:34:34,900 --> 00:34:40,580
And in terms of this objective,
if we have a good advantage

644
00:34:40,580 --> 00:34:44,820
function, and we go back
to the stitching example,

645
00:34:44,820 --> 00:34:48,659
for this particular
state, right here,

646
00:34:48,659 --> 00:34:56,969
we know that the advantage of
this action is high, right?

647
00:34:56,969 --> 00:35:01,730
Because the value, the
Q-value of this is 1.

648
00:35:01,730 --> 00:35:06,250
The Q-value for going this way,
at least in the data, is 0,

649
00:35:06,250 --> 00:35:11,010
and the value of this state
will be the average of those two

650
00:35:11,010 --> 00:35:12,250
outcomes.

651
00:35:12,250 --> 00:35:14,470
And so the advantage for
this one will be positive.

652
00:35:14,470 --> 00:35:19,370
And the advantage for going
this direction will be negative.

653
00:35:19,370 --> 00:35:22,810
And so when we then take our
imitation learning objective

654
00:35:22,810 --> 00:35:25,090
and weight it by the
advantages, we're

655
00:35:25,090 --> 00:35:27,650
going to be telling our
policy in that state,

656
00:35:27,650 --> 00:35:30,010
it should go to the
good outcome rather than

657
00:35:30,010 --> 00:35:32,130
going to the bad outcome.

658
00:35:32,130 --> 00:35:35,350
And so this objective, if you
have a good advantage estimate,

659
00:35:35,350 --> 00:35:38,570
is capable of stitching
together good behaviors

660
00:35:38,570 --> 00:35:40,833
in an offline data set.

661
00:35:40,833 --> 00:35:42,250
And so in terms
of the derivation,

662
00:35:42,250 --> 00:35:45,610
you can show that this advantage
weighted imitation learning

663
00:35:45,610 --> 00:35:51,370
objective is approximating
a KL constraint objective.

664
00:35:51,370 --> 00:35:58,010
Where you can show that
it's maximizing the future

665
00:35:58,010 --> 00:36:01,250
rewards, as measured by your key
value, subject to the constraint

666
00:36:01,250 --> 00:36:05,250
that you're staying close
to your behavior policy.

667
00:36:05,250 --> 00:36:10,090
And this is a pretty reasonable
objective for offline RL.

668
00:36:10,090 --> 00:36:12,650
Because if you're too far
from your behavior policy,

669
00:36:12,650 --> 00:36:17,130
then you won't be able to--

670
00:36:17,130 --> 00:36:21,250
you won't be able to actually
get good information about how

671
00:36:21,250 --> 00:36:22,220
to improve your policy.

672
00:36:22,220 --> 00:36:27,090

673
00:36:27,090 --> 00:36:31,290
So before we talk about how
we estimate the advantage

674
00:36:31,290 --> 00:36:34,330
and what this advantage
will be, any questions

675
00:36:34,330 --> 00:36:36,770
on what this
objective looks like?

676
00:36:36,770 --> 00:36:38,570
Yeah, so the
question is for this

677
00:36:38,570 --> 00:36:40,270
to improve over
imitation learning,

678
00:36:40,270 --> 00:36:43,010
do we need to assume that
our trajectories have

679
00:36:43,010 --> 00:36:44,490
intersecting states?

680
00:36:44,490 --> 00:36:49,977
And the brief answer is
that, no, we generally

681
00:36:49,977 --> 00:36:51,060
don't have to assume that.

682
00:36:51,060 --> 00:36:52,840
And in this diagram,
I actually tried

683
00:36:52,840 --> 00:36:57,080
to have S7 and S3 not be
exactly the same state.

684
00:36:57,080 --> 00:36:59,880
And the reason why we don't
need them to be exactly the same

685
00:36:59,880 --> 00:37:03,360
is that if we're using a
neural network to represent

686
00:37:03,360 --> 00:37:06,920
the policy and the
advantage function,

687
00:37:06,920 --> 00:37:09,628
then it will naturally--

688
00:37:09,628 --> 00:37:11,920
States and actions that are
very similar to each other,

689
00:37:11,920 --> 00:37:14,320
will naturally have
similar outputs

690
00:37:14,320 --> 00:37:15,740
from that neural network.

691
00:37:15,740 --> 00:37:18,760
And so it will be able to--

692
00:37:18,760 --> 00:37:21,640
it should be able to
generalize, if the states

693
00:37:21,640 --> 00:37:24,360
are similar to each other,
but not exactly intersecting.

694
00:37:24,360 --> 00:37:29,000
Now, the degree to this how
well this works will depend on

695
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:32,520
how close those states are
and how large the data set

696
00:37:32,520 --> 00:37:34,520
is, such that it can learn
a representation that

697
00:37:34,520 --> 00:37:37,680
is collapsing those
representations to something

698
00:37:37,680 --> 00:37:39,422
that looks similar.

699
00:37:39,422 --> 00:37:40,880
The question is,
in practice, do we

700
00:37:40,880 --> 00:37:44,400
need to worry about the
exponential term exploding?

701
00:37:44,400 --> 00:37:47,080
When we look at a practical
implementation of this,

702
00:37:47,080 --> 00:37:50,920
we will actually add a
hyperparameter that corresponds

703
00:37:50,920 --> 00:37:53,160
to the temperature of this.

704
00:37:53,160 --> 00:38:01,840
And so the practical algorithm
will have 1 over alpha times

705
00:38:01,840 --> 00:38:03,340
the advantage.

706
00:38:03,340 --> 00:38:07,550
And that can help prevent this
from getting extremely large.

707
00:38:07,550 --> 00:38:10,100

708
00:38:10,100 --> 00:38:10,600
Yeah.

709
00:38:10,600 --> 00:38:13,500
And then, I guess
it depends a bit--

710
00:38:13,500 --> 00:38:16,403

711
00:38:16,403 --> 00:38:18,320
maybe we'll talk about
it also a bit more when

712
00:38:18,320 --> 00:38:20,880
we get into fitting advantages.

713
00:38:20,880 --> 00:38:23,020
Yeah, I knew this
question would come up.

714
00:38:23,020 --> 00:38:25,880
So when we talked about
actor critic methods,

715
00:38:25,880 --> 00:38:28,365
the objective looked
similar to this,

716
00:38:28,365 --> 00:38:29,740
except instead of
exponentiating,

717
00:38:29,740 --> 00:38:31,400
we just had the advantage here.

718
00:38:31,400 --> 00:38:33,560
The other key difference
was that here, we're

719
00:38:33,560 --> 00:38:36,260
sampling from our
offline data set.

720
00:38:36,260 --> 00:38:40,320
And in the actor-critic
objectives,

721
00:38:40,320 --> 00:38:42,880
the action, well, there's
different versions,

722
00:38:42,880 --> 00:38:45,000
but we're sampling
from an online data set

723
00:38:45,000 --> 00:38:49,350
data set, typically, and
in soft actor-critic,

724
00:38:49,350 --> 00:38:51,450
this action actually
comes from your policy,

725
00:38:51,450 --> 00:38:54,910
not from the data set.

726
00:38:54,910 --> 00:39:00,990
There isn't a very quick
way to derive where

727
00:39:00,990 --> 00:39:04,470
those differences come from.

728
00:39:04,470 --> 00:39:08,250
One, I don't want
to confuse you,

729
00:39:08,250 --> 00:39:12,390
but the one way of
thinking about this

730
00:39:12,390 --> 00:39:16,990
is, oftentimes, online RL
objectives will be optimizing

731
00:39:16,990 --> 00:39:19,590
a reverse scale objective,
and offline are looking more

732
00:39:19,590 --> 00:39:21,050
at a forward tail objective.

733
00:39:21,050 --> 00:39:25,010
But it's a longer explanation.

734
00:39:25,010 --> 00:39:26,310
Yeah.

735
00:39:26,310 --> 00:39:28,150
OK.

736
00:39:28,150 --> 00:39:32,310
And there's also some
references here as well.

737
00:39:32,310 --> 00:39:34,910
So we've talked
about this advantage.

738
00:39:34,910 --> 00:39:39,270
Now, advantage actually
have some policy

739
00:39:39,270 --> 00:39:42,110
associated with them.

740
00:39:42,110 --> 00:39:46,310
It's the advantage of
taking action a in state S,

741
00:39:46,310 --> 00:39:48,590
and then following some policy.

742
00:39:48,590 --> 00:39:50,310
And so the question is--

743
00:39:50,310 --> 00:39:53,310
the first question is,
which policy are we trying

744
00:39:53,310 --> 00:39:56,190
to estimate the advantage for?

745
00:39:56,190 --> 00:39:59,990
For now, we can try to estimate
the advantage of the behavior

746
00:39:59,990 --> 00:40:02,830
policy.

747
00:40:02,830 --> 00:40:04,590
And then a key
question comes up is,

748
00:40:04,590 --> 00:40:08,428
how do we want to estimate this
advantage function, ideally,

749
00:40:08,428 --> 00:40:10,470
in a way that prevents
the overestimation that we

750
00:40:10,470 --> 00:40:11,040
saw before.

751
00:40:11,040 --> 00:40:14,310

752
00:40:14,310 --> 00:40:19,790
So the first way that we
can estimate the advantage

753
00:40:19,790 --> 00:40:21,450
function--

754
00:40:21,450 --> 00:40:23,270
I mean, generally,
it will look very

755
00:40:23,270 --> 00:40:26,790
similar to what we saw before,
when estimating the advantage

756
00:40:26,790 --> 00:40:27,290
function.

757
00:40:27,290 --> 00:40:29,830
But there's a couple
other considerations.

758
00:40:29,830 --> 00:40:33,510
So let's say version--

759
00:40:33,510 --> 00:40:36,210
maybe I'll do this
in a new color.

760
00:40:36,210 --> 00:40:44,260
So let's say version 1, it's v1.

761
00:40:44,260 --> 00:40:47,380
Version 1, we're going
to use the simplest

762
00:40:47,380 --> 00:40:51,100
thing that we had before, which
is to use Monte Carlo returns.

763
00:40:51,100 --> 00:40:53,350
So we can first fit
a value function.

764
00:40:53,350 --> 00:40:56,140

765
00:40:56,140 --> 00:40:59,100
And because we're fitting the
value function for the behavior

766
00:40:59,100 --> 00:41:03,540
policy, we don't need to--

767
00:41:03,540 --> 00:41:04,340
it is coming--

768
00:41:04,340 --> 00:41:05,757
The data is coming
from the policy

769
00:41:05,757 --> 00:41:08,620
that we're trying to evaluate
a value function for.

770
00:41:08,620 --> 00:41:14,420
So if we want to fit a value
function with Monte Carlo

771
00:41:14,420 --> 00:41:18,540
returns, that our
objective looks--

772
00:41:18,540 --> 00:41:24,450
we're trying to regress to
the sum of future rewards.

773
00:41:24,450 --> 00:41:26,980

774
00:41:26,980 --> 00:41:29,650
So our objective looks
something like this.

775
00:41:29,650 --> 00:41:33,340

776
00:41:33,340 --> 00:41:35,300
So this is something
that basically supervised

777
00:41:35,300 --> 00:41:38,180
learning to the future rewards.

778
00:41:38,180 --> 00:41:42,440
And then, once we do this,
which is fairly straightforward,

779
00:41:42,440 --> 00:41:46,900
then the question is, how do we
get the advantage out of this?

780
00:41:46,900 --> 00:41:49,220
There's a couple ways
you could do this.

781
00:41:49,220 --> 00:41:53,160
In the first time
that we saw this,

782
00:41:53,160 --> 00:41:58,220
the way that we got
the advantage function

783
00:41:58,220 --> 00:42:08,660
for a given state and action was
to use the reward plus the value

784
00:42:08,660 --> 00:42:13,580
function at the next time
step minus the value function

785
00:42:13,580 --> 00:42:15,080
at the current time step.

786
00:42:15,080 --> 00:42:21,540
And these are all the value
function that we just estimated.

787
00:42:21,540 --> 00:42:22,490
So you could use this.

788
00:42:22,490 --> 00:42:25,260

789
00:42:25,260 --> 00:42:29,900
The one alternative--
so you could use this.

790
00:42:29,900 --> 00:42:32,660
This is going to rely on
having a value function that

791
00:42:32,660 --> 00:42:36,820
