for a policy that's better

1005
00:53:47,960 --> 00:53:50,360
than the behavior policy.

1006
00:53:50,360 --> 00:53:53,040
And one of the ways
to think about this

1007
00:53:53,040 --> 00:53:55,960
is that, when we're
trying to minimize

1008
00:53:55,960 --> 00:53:59,820
this bootstrapped objective,
this target, right here,

1009
00:53:59,820 --> 00:54:04,880
this is a sample
of v of s prime.

1010
00:54:04,880 --> 00:54:09,520
So like we saw
here, v of s prime,

1011
00:54:09,520 --> 00:54:13,040
we're estimating the
mean, the average

1012
00:54:13,040 --> 00:54:18,030
of the outcomes, the average
of the expected future rewards.

1013
00:54:18,030 --> 00:54:20,710
But actually, there's a
distribution of outcomes

1014
00:54:20,710 --> 00:54:24,390
that's represented in the data.

1015
00:54:24,390 --> 00:54:29,870
And so if we think about
that distribution--

1016
00:54:29,870 --> 00:54:33,550
so we have our value function.

1017
00:54:33,550 --> 00:54:36,350
And if we thought about
the value function

1018
00:54:36,350 --> 00:54:41,950
for a given state, when you
are taking different actions,

1019
00:54:41,950 --> 00:54:45,230
you see different
sums of rewards.

1020
00:54:45,230 --> 00:54:52,710
And so there's actually
some underlying distribution

1021
00:54:52,710 --> 00:54:56,650
for that in your data,
for a given state.

1022
00:54:56,650 --> 00:54:59,150
So one state-- or
maybe a bunch of states

1023
00:54:59,150 --> 00:55:07,090
see a reward that that's
like, at this value,

1024
00:55:07,090 --> 00:55:10,470
maybe a smaller number have a
higher sum of rewards in them

1025
00:55:10,470 --> 00:55:11,910
and so forth.

1026
00:55:11,910 --> 00:55:15,230
And then when we do
Monte Carlo regression,

1027
00:55:15,230 --> 00:55:20,190
if this distribution has
a mean, maybe, right here,

1028
00:55:20,190 --> 00:55:24,270
then this is what
when we do when

1029
00:55:24,270 --> 00:55:26,750
we're trying to fit
like the Monte Carlo

1030
00:55:26,750 --> 00:55:31,810
estimate for our
behavior policy,

1031
00:55:31,810 --> 00:55:33,330
then we get this
average right here.

1032
00:55:33,330 --> 00:55:38,470
We're averaging over the
rewards that we're seeing.

1033
00:55:38,470 --> 00:55:41,510
Now, if we wanted to
try to learn a value

1034
00:55:41,510 --> 00:55:46,550
function for a policy
that's better than pi beta,

1035
00:55:46,550 --> 00:55:47,670
we could then--

1036
00:55:47,670 --> 00:55:49,230
we could think
about, maybe, instead

1037
00:55:49,230 --> 00:55:54,230
of trying to fit the mean
of the outcomes that we see,

1038
00:55:54,230 --> 00:55:57,750
maybe we could try to fit
some other percentile.

1039
00:55:57,750 --> 00:56:02,830
So this is the mean,
but maybe, if instead,

1040
00:56:02,830 --> 00:56:07,550
we could try to fit the 70th
percentile or something like

1041
00:56:07,550 --> 00:56:10,670
that, then maybe we could
actually get a value function

1042
00:56:10,670 --> 00:56:14,870
for a policy that is better
than our behavior policy.

1043
00:56:14,870 --> 00:56:18,060

1044
00:56:18,060 --> 00:56:20,892
So how do we do that?

1045
00:56:20,892 --> 00:56:22,600
Maybe let's go through
this on the slide.

1046
00:56:22,600 --> 00:56:25,740
So we have-- when we're
trying to estimate

1047
00:56:25,740 --> 00:56:31,200
rewards plus future Q-functions,
this is a sample of the values.

1048
00:56:31,200 --> 00:56:33,940
If we think about a histogram
of the values for a given

1049
00:56:33,940 --> 00:56:38,760
state, when we are
using this target,

1050
00:56:38,760 --> 00:56:44,260
we're using an
average of the value.

1051
00:56:44,260 --> 00:56:48,940
Also, the l2 loss right here
is giving us that value.

1052
00:56:48,940 --> 00:56:51,240
Now, the Vs for the policy--

1053
00:56:51,240 --> 00:56:53,540
the best policy in the
support of the data

1054
00:56:53,540 --> 00:56:56,380
is going to be right here.

1055
00:56:56,380 --> 00:56:59,380
And so maybe, there's
some way to try

1056
00:56:59,380 --> 00:57:03,340
to use a different loss
function, other than the l2 loss

1057
00:57:03,340 --> 00:57:04,490
to get a better policy.

1058
00:57:04,490 --> 00:57:08,660

1059
00:57:08,660 --> 00:57:11,000
Now, an idea for how to do this.

1060
00:57:11,000 --> 00:57:23,110
So when we use an l2
loss, we have some error.

1061
00:57:23,110 --> 00:57:28,580

1062
00:57:28,580 --> 00:57:32,880
And so this is the error
plotted on the x-axis.

1063
00:57:32,880 --> 00:57:36,660
And then l2 loss, we
have something like this,

1064
00:57:36,660 --> 00:57:39,660
where maybe let's call this e.

1065
00:57:39,660 --> 00:57:42,900
This is e squared.

1066
00:57:42,900 --> 00:57:45,650
And if we instead
wanted to get--

1067
00:57:45,650 --> 00:57:49,460

1068
00:57:49,460 --> 00:57:54,580
if we instead wanted to try to
get a value estimate for this,

1069
00:57:54,580 --> 00:57:56,580
what we could do is,
if we instead of having

1070
00:57:56,580 --> 00:58:00,060
this symmetric loss that is--

1071
00:58:00,060 --> 00:58:01,000
yeah, symmetric.

1072
00:58:01,000 --> 00:58:03,220
If you're less than
0 and greater than 0,

1073
00:58:03,220 --> 00:58:15,370
you can instead use a loss
function that looks like this.

1074
00:58:15,370 --> 00:58:19,490
And you can actually show
that loss functions like this

1075
00:58:19,490 --> 00:58:23,770
will actually instead
try-- basically,

1076
00:58:23,770 --> 00:58:25,690
you'll be penalized
less for making errors

1077
00:58:25,690 --> 00:58:27,410
in a particular direction.

1078
00:58:27,410 --> 00:58:33,730
And this will actually
give you a value function

1079
00:58:33,730 --> 00:58:36,730
that looks more like this,
that's actually better

1080
00:58:36,730 --> 00:58:40,290
than your behavior policy.

1081
00:58:40,290 --> 00:58:42,370
Now, specifically,
what this looks like

1082
00:58:42,370 --> 00:58:46,770
is, if you think about--

1083
00:58:46,770 --> 00:58:48,850
you have some loss function.

1084
00:58:48,850 --> 00:58:52,970
We'll call this--
there'll be some--

1085
00:58:52,970 --> 00:58:55,570
maybe this is respect to error.

1086
00:58:55,570 --> 00:58:57,850
This loss function is going
to behave differently,

1087
00:58:57,850 --> 00:59:04,090
if the error is less than 0,
versus if the error is greater

1088
00:59:04,090 --> 00:59:05,450
than 0.

1089
00:59:05,450 --> 00:59:11,230
And let me just remember
what the direction is.

1090
00:59:11,230 --> 00:59:12,450
So if it's less than 0--

1091
00:59:12,450 --> 00:59:14,020
so we will have--

1092
00:59:14,020 --> 00:59:16,610

1093
00:59:16,610 --> 00:59:21,250
and the base case is just
x squared or e squared.

1094
00:59:21,250 --> 00:59:23,370
Then, we'll have some
hyperparameter that

1095
00:59:23,370 --> 00:59:26,970
weights our loss by some value.

1096
00:59:26,970 --> 00:59:33,570
And if lambda is 0.5, then this
is the typical l2 objective,

1097
00:59:33,570 --> 00:59:37,090
because it will be e squared,
both less than 0 and above 0.

1098
00:59:37,090 --> 00:59:41,290
And if lambda is less
than 0.5, then it

1099
00:59:41,290 --> 00:59:46,970
will be downweighting the
loss, when it's greater than 0

1100
00:59:46,970 --> 00:59:51,370
and upweighting it when it is--

1101
00:59:51,370 --> 00:59:52,392
sorry.

1102
00:59:52,392 --> 00:59:53,850
Downweighting it
with it's above 0,

1103
00:59:53,850 --> 00:59:55,558
and upweighting it
when it's less than 0.

1104
00:59:55,558 --> 01:00:05,375
And the reverse will be true,
when lambda is greater than 0.5.

1105
01:00:05,375 --> 01:00:07,000
So what this looks
like is instead of--

1106
01:00:07,000 --> 01:00:08,860
I guess I drew it
slightly wrong there.

1107
01:00:08,860 --> 01:00:11,068
Instead of using a loss
function, it looks like this.

1108
01:00:11,068 --> 01:00:12,760
You can use an
asymmetric loss function

1109
01:00:12,760 --> 01:00:20,000
that is penalizing errors
less in one direction.

1110
01:00:20,000 --> 01:00:22,240
This is called
expected regression.

1111
01:00:22,240 --> 01:00:24,000
It's a cool trick.

1112
01:00:24,000 --> 01:00:27,400
You can think about
it in terms of rather

1113
01:00:27,400 --> 01:00:32,120
than just error, some
arbitrary random variable x.

1114
01:00:32,120 --> 01:00:36,920
And if you define different
values of this lambda term,

1115
01:00:36,920 --> 01:00:39,720
then you get these
asymmetric losses

1116
01:00:39,720 --> 01:00:43,640
that look like this,
where, again, if lambda

1117
01:00:43,640 --> 01:00:46,307
is greater than
0.5, then you see

1118
01:00:46,307 --> 01:00:48,640
it as asymmetric in this
direction versus, if it's less,

1119
01:00:48,640 --> 01:00:50,740
you see asymmetric in
the other direction.

1120
01:00:50,740 --> 01:00:54,080

1121
01:00:54,080 --> 01:00:55,560
And here's another
visualization,

1122
01:00:55,560 --> 01:00:57,880
where if you're trying to--

1123
01:00:57,880 --> 01:01:01,980
the mean of this random variable
is shown in the red curve,

1124
01:01:01,980 --> 01:01:04,120
but then, you can
get different tiles

1125
01:01:04,120 --> 01:01:05,790
with different values of lambda.

1126
01:01:05,790 --> 01:01:09,000

1127
01:01:09,000 --> 01:01:12,640
So then coming back
to our algorithms,

1128
01:01:12,640 --> 01:01:17,480
if we want to fit
our value function,

1129
01:01:17,480 --> 01:01:22,800
we can use this expected
loss and basically

1130
01:01:22,800 --> 01:01:25,680
fit a value function
that is trying

1131
01:01:25,680 --> 01:01:27,640
to estimate a value
function that's

1132
01:01:27,640 --> 01:01:29,360
better than-- for
a policy, that's

1133
01:01:29,360 --> 01:01:31,000
better than a behavior policy.

1134
01:01:31,000 --> 01:01:33,800
And this will be penalizing
you for overestimating

1135
01:01:33,800 --> 01:01:35,520
and underestimating,
but it won't

1136
01:01:35,520 --> 01:01:39,392
be penalizing you as much, if
you overestimate a little bit.

1137
01:01:39,392 --> 01:01:41,100
Basically, if you're
off by a little bit.

1138
01:01:41,100 --> 01:01:44,320
And this means that when you
have these different outcomes,

1139
01:01:44,320 --> 01:01:49,780
and these different outcomes
right here, if you get the mean,

1140
01:01:49,780 --> 01:01:50,840
you get 3.

1141
01:01:50,840 --> 01:01:54,160
Whereas, if you have--

1142
01:01:54,160 --> 01:01:57,740
I haven't written down exactly
what the optimum would be.

1143
01:01:57,740 --> 01:02:05,230
But for some value of lambda,
it'll be optimal instead.

1144
01:02:05,230 --> 01:02:07,230
Instead of outputting
the mean, it'll

1145
01:02:07,230 --> 01:02:09,110
be more optimal to
output something

1146
01:02:09,110 --> 01:02:11,990
like 6, for example,
where you're

1147
01:02:11,990 --> 01:02:17,670
penalized less for
overestimating this value.

1148
01:02:17,670 --> 01:02:19,240
And yeah, the
outcome of the loss

1149
01:02:19,240 --> 01:02:20,990
function when you use
some things together

1150
01:02:20,990 --> 01:02:23,730
will be something that's
higher than the mean.

1151
01:02:23,730 --> 01:02:28,008

1152
01:02:28,008 --> 01:02:29,550
So in particular,
for this algorithm,

1153
01:02:29,550 --> 01:02:31,930
we'll use the expected loss
to fit the value function.

1154
01:02:31,930 --> 01:02:34,470
We'll actually also
fit a Q-function

1155
01:02:34,470 --> 01:02:37,670
with the typical mean
squared error loss.

1156
01:02:37,670 --> 01:02:42,790
And we'll actually use the
value function as the target.

1157
01:02:42,790 --> 01:02:46,430
So we'll be fitting both the
value function and a Q-function.

1158
01:02:46,430 --> 01:02:48,750
And we'll just be using
this expected loss

1159
01:02:48,750 --> 01:02:51,030
for fitting the value function.

1160
01:02:51,030 --> 01:02:55,230
And then once we have our
Q-function and value function,

1161
01:02:55,230 --> 01:02:58,550
then we can estimate
the advantages Q minus V

1162
01:02:58,550 --> 01:03:03,550
and use the same AWR method
to get a policy, where

1163
01:03:03,550 --> 01:03:05,270
we're weighting
on the likelihoods

1164
01:03:05,270 --> 01:03:10,615
by the exponentiated Q minus V.

1165
01:03:10,615 --> 01:03:11,990
Yeah, so the
question is, will we

1166
01:03:11,990 --> 01:03:14,370
be overestimating the
value in this case?

1167
01:03:14,370 --> 01:03:17,030
So one thing to note
about this algorithm

1168
01:03:17,030 --> 01:03:20,990
is, we're never going to be
passing as input actions that

1169
01:03:20,990 --> 01:03:22,590
are not in the data.

1170
01:03:22,590 --> 01:03:25,070
And that was one of the major
sources of overestimation

1171
01:03:25,070 --> 01:03:26,270
before.

1172
01:03:26,270 --> 01:03:28,410
Now depending on
how you set lambda,

1173
01:03:28,410 --> 01:03:34,390
if you set lambda to be 0, then
you will overestimate probably.

1174
01:03:34,390 --> 01:03:37,230
And typically, you'll
choose some value

1175
01:03:37,230 --> 01:03:40,590
that's maybe 0.2
or 0.2, something

1176
01:03:40,590 --> 01:03:44,550
like that's more in the middle
that's not so aggressive.

1177
01:03:44,550 --> 01:03:50,375
And you will be getting an
overestimate of the pi beta.

1178
01:03:50,375 --> 01:03:51,750
And the reason
for that is you're

1179
01:03:51,750 --> 01:03:54,083
actually trying to estimate
the value of a policy that's

1180
01:03:54,083 --> 01:03:55,230
better than pi beta.

1181
01:03:55,230 --> 01:03:59,780
So you're trying to estimate.

1182
01:03:59,780 --> 01:04:07,740
V pi for a policy pi
that's better than pi beta.

1183
01:04:07,740 --> 01:04:13,380
So you're purposefully
overestimating the pi beta.

1184
01:04:13,380 --> 01:04:16,060
The question is, because we're
going to be gradually improving

1185
01:04:16,060 --> 01:04:20,220
our policy, would it make sense
to gradually decrease lambda?

1186
01:04:20,220 --> 01:04:23,360
So we're not actually
collecting data from our policy.

1187
01:04:23,360 --> 01:04:27,740
And so all the data is coming
from our offline data set.

1188
01:04:27,740 --> 01:04:31,255
And in that case, it makes
sense to keep it constant

1189
01:04:31,255 --> 01:04:33,380
because you always want to
be a fixed amount better

1190
01:04:33,380 --> 01:04:34,900
than your behavior policy.

1191
01:04:34,900 --> 01:04:36,660
Now, if you did in
principle actually

1192
01:04:36,660 --> 01:04:39,520
do start collecting
some online data,

1193
01:04:39,520 --> 01:04:43,180
then things will change a
little bit, because then you'll

1194
01:04:43,180 --> 01:04:47,400
actually be skewing your
data set to a better policy.

1195
01:04:47,400 --> 01:04:49,900
And in that case, it actually
might make sense, potentially,

1196
01:04:49,900 --> 01:04:54,700
to bring lambda closer to
0, or sorry, closer to 0.5.

1197
01:04:54,700 --> 01:04:57,300
Because you'll then have
data from a better policy--

1198
01:04:57,300 --> 01:05:00,740
a policy better than
your behavior policy.

1199
01:05:00,740 --> 01:05:02,420
So pros of this
algorithm, we don't

1200
01:05:02,420 --> 01:05:04,440
need to query out of
distribution actions.

1201
01:05:04,440 --> 01:05:06,980
The policy is still only
trained on actions in the data.

1202
01:05:06,980 --> 01:05:09,880
And we're decoupling
actor and critic training.

1203
01:05:09,880 --> 01:05:12,200
So we just do these
critic training.

1204
01:05:12,200 --> 01:05:15,202
We're fitting a value function
and a Q-function on our data.

1205
01:05:15,202 --> 01:05:16,660
And then once we're
done with that,

1206
01:05:16,660 --> 01:05:19,980
and that's converged, or
we've done enough training,

1207
01:05:19,980 --> 01:05:21,830
then we train our policy.

1208
01:05:21,830 --> 01:05:24,860

1209
01:05:24,860 --> 01:05:25,500
Cool.

1210
01:05:25,500 --> 01:05:28,160
So this is an algorithm
called implicit Q-learning.

1211
01:05:28,160 --> 01:05:30,160
Yeah, so the question is
where are we fitting Q?

1212
01:05:30,160 --> 01:05:33,460
So we could also use Monte
Carlo returns with this

1213
01:05:33,460 --> 01:05:39,100
and use the spectral-like
objective with this term.

1214
01:05:39,100 --> 01:05:44,380
It's nice to use Q because Q
is lower variance than this sum

1215
01:05:44,380 --> 01:05:46,460
of rewards right here.

1216
01:05:46,460 --> 01:05:53,370
And we can fit a Q-function,
using the bootstrapped backups.

1217
01:05:53,370 --> 01:05:55,450
So all these ideas are
applicable to Monte Carlo

1218
01:05:55,450 --> 01:05:56,810
as well.

1219
01:05:56,810 --> 01:05:59,050
But using Q-functions,
like when you

1220
01:05:59,050 --> 01:06:02,050
can use this bootstrapped
objective, which you could only

1221
01:06:02,050 --> 01:06:06,250
do with Q-functions, then you'll
get a lower variance target.

1222
01:06:06,250 --> 01:06:08,990
And then I guess to summarize
the point of offline RL.

1223
01:06:08,990 --> 01:06:11,090
So online data is
really expensive,

1224
01:06:11,090 --> 01:06:14,170
because we have to recollect it
every time we run our algorithm.

1225
01:06:14,170 --> 01:06:18,170
And it's really, really
useful to reuse offline data.

1226
01:06:18,170 --> 01:06:19,890
One of the big challenges
with offline RL

1227
01:06:19,890 --> 01:06:21,810
is this shift
between your behavior

1228
01:06:21,810 --> 01:06:23,730
policy and your learn policy.

1229
01:06:23,730 --> 01:06:27,410
And we talked about a
filtered or weighted imitation

1230
01:06:27,410 --> 01:06:29,410
learning method as a baseline.

1231
01:06:29,410 --> 01:06:32,330
And we talked about how we can
actually explicitly constrain

1232
01:06:32,330 --> 01:06:34,930
the policy to the
behavior policy

1233
01:06:34,930 --> 01:06:38,850
by only supervising the policy
on actions in your data.

1234
01:06:38,850 --> 01:06:41,810
And so that allowed us
to get rid or move away

1235
01:06:41,810 --> 01:06:45,410
from this distribution
shift problem.

1236
01:06:45,410 --> 01:06:47,450
And then the trajectory
stitching idea

1237
01:06:47,450 --> 01:06:49,770
that we talked about before
allows us to improve over

1238
01:06:49,770 --> 01:06:53,610
vanilla imitation learning.

1239
01:06:53,610 --> 01:06:57,130
This example application is for
the algorithm we didn't cover.

1240
01:06:57,130 --> 01:06:59,370
If you want to
train offline filter

1241
01:06:59,370 --> 01:07:01,450
behavior cloning and
implicit Q-learning

1242
01:07:01,450 --> 01:07:04,490
are both good approaches,
but implicit Q-learning

1243
01:07:04,490 --> 01:07:07,970
is going to be probably
work better, if you have,

1244
01:07:07,970 --> 01:07:10,460
especially if you have
a broader data set.

1245
01:07:10,460 --> 01:07:12,210
And if you want to do
offline pre-training

1246
01:07:12,210 --> 01:07:16,090
and online fine-tuning, IQL is
actually a very good algorithm

1247
01:07:16,090 --> 01:07:16,790
for doing that.

1248
01:07:16,790 --> 01:07:20,090
It can also be used with
online data collection

1249
01:07:20,090 --> 01:07:22,730
quite effectively, and it
works quite well there.

1250
01:07:22,730 --> 01:07:24,850
There's also a
variant of IQL called

1251
01:07:24,850 --> 01:07:29,850
IDQL that uses diffusion
policies for your policy class

1252
01:07:29,850 --> 01:07:31,410
right here.

1253
01:07:31,410 --> 01:07:33,620
And it also works quite well.

1254
01:07:33,620 --> 01:07:35,370
And so if you're
interested in diving more

1255
01:07:35,370 --> 01:07:37,410
into IQL-like algorithms,
I would encourage

1256
01:07:37,410 --> 01:07:39,250
you to check out IDQL.

1257
01:07:39,250 --> 01:07:41,470
It is actually--
beyond using diffusion,

1258
01:07:41,470 --> 01:07:43,010
it also is a bit
different in terms

1259
01:07:43,010 --> 01:07:45,480
of how this update is done.

